    {
      "segment_id": "S00001",
      "start_char": 0,
      "end_char": 210,
      "text": "MEXICO\nWith oil prices expected to decline next year, the formula underpinning the fuel pricing mechanism\nis expected to result in lower subsidies in 2023. The authorities pointed to a track record of tapering\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00002",
      "start_char": 210,
      "end_char": 400,
      "text": "subsidies when market price pressures abated. Other policies addressing food prices include the\nremoval of tariffs on key food imports and regulatory agreements with retailers on food price\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00003",
      "start_char": 400,
      "end_char": 594,
      "text": "passthrough. However, in anticipation of possible downside risks, they are interested in reforms to\nthe fiscal framework that would enable the allocation of fiscal savings and funding from debt\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00004",
      "start_char": 594,
      "end_char": 794,
      "text": "issuance to the stabilization fund, while respecting key tenets of the current framework. In case of a\nshock affecting vulnerable population, the authorities consider they can create space to provide\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00005",
      "start_char": 794,
      "end_char": 1032,
      "text": "support by reallocating spending away from public investment.\nExternal Sector Policies\n32. The peso has faced less\ndownward pressure over the past year\nthan other emerging market currencies.\nThe relatively high interest rate differential\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00006",
      "start_char": 1032,
      "end_char": 1236,
      "text": "between Mexico and the U.S. has made\nholding peso assets more attractive, which\nhas helped in containing upside risks to\ninflation. In addition, the peso has been\nsupported by strong remittances, a track\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00007",
      "start_char": 1236,
      "end_char": 1469,
      "text": "record of fiscal discipline, and some\ninflows linked to the “nearshoring” of\nproductive capacity.\n33. In the event of a weakening of the\nbalance of payments, peso depreciation should 8\nbe allowed to act as a shock absorber. Standard\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00008",
      "start_char": 1469,
      "end_char": 1671,
      "text": "financial frictions underpinning concerns about 6\ndepreciation generally are not relevant in Mexico’s\n4\ncase. In particular, peso foreign exchange (FX)\nmarkets are deep and liquid and even during the\n2\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00009",
      "start_char": 1671,
      "end_char": 1871,
      "text": "turbulence in 2020 saw below-average rises in the\nUIP premium and in bid-ask spreads. FX\n0\nmismatches in balance sheets are contained. The\neconomy and financial sector (see below) are thus -2\n2 4 6 8\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00010",
      "start_char": 1871,
      "end_char": 2070,
      "text": "expected to remain resilient if downward pressure\non the peso materialized. However, in the case of a\nlarge shock resulting in sizeable depreciation, policy rate hikes might be needed to counter the\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00011",
      "start_char": 2070,
      "end_char": 2270,
      "text": "inflation passthrough. If such a shock triggered market illiquidity and sharply higher bid-ask spreads,\ntemporary FX intervention could be considered. The IMF’s Flexible Credit Line (FCL) provides an\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00012",
      "start_char": 2270,
      "end_char": 2483,
      "text": "additional external buffer against such external risks and will help contribute to market confidence.\n18 INTERNATIONAL MONETARY FUND\n.S.U\neht\nhtiw\nlaitnereffiD\netaR\nnoitalfnI\nExhange Rates Against the U.S. Dollar\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00013",
      "start_char": 2483,
      "end_char": 2720,
      "text": "(Index: Jan 2020 = 100; increase = appreciation)\n110\n105\n100\n95\n90\n85\n80\n75\n70\n65\n60\nJan-20 Jul-20 Jan-21 Jul-21 Jan-22 Jul-22\nMexico Peru Colombia Chile Brasil\nSource: Bloombergand IMF staff calculations.\nInflation and Policy Rate Gaps\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00014",
      "start_char": 2720,
      "end_char": 2794,
      "text": "2009-2021\n2022\nPolicy Rate Differential with U.S.\nSource: Haver Analytics."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "schema_version": "locator_index_v1",
  "source_id": "MAC-010",
  "source_sha256": "8fac4146590dbe2ed458f7356748f5880d021e03cc4982b1e7efe083d33f622a",
  "locator": "page 23",
  "segments": [
    {
      "segment_id": "S00001",
      "start_char": 0,
      "end_char": 211,
      "text": "MEXICO\nAuthorities’ Views\n34. The authorities consider that the country’s external buffers served them well during\nthe pandemic and in the recent tightening period. The flexible exchange rate helped support the\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00002",
      "start_char": 211,
      "end_char": 412,
      "text": "recovery by stimulating manufacturing. Portfolio outflows have proved manageable in the context of\nsubstantial remittances and recently resurgent FDI, while the level of foreign participation in local\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00003",
      "start_char": 412,
      "end_char": 639,
      "text": "currency sovereign bond market, now back to earlier levels, lowers risks to future external shocks.\nC. Maintaining a Robust Financial System\n35. The financial system has emerged from the pandemic in good health. Non-performing\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00004",
      "start_char": 639,
      "end_char": 836,
      "text": "loans (NPLs) rose modestly from low levels during the pandemic and began declining last year.\nCapital adequacy ratios are high and rising, and liquidity ratios are comfortable. With these buffers,\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00005",
      "start_char": 836,
      "end_char": 1032,
      "text": "the banking system demonstrated strong resilience to severe macrofinancial shocks in the solvency\nand liquidity stress tests conducted under the 2022 FSAP (although some smaller banks may require\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00006",
      "start_char": 1032,
      "end_char": 1238,
      "text": "additional buffers to handle such stresses). Overall, systemic vulnerabilities and liquidity risks in the\nfinancial system appear broadly contained, given high capital buffers, low private sector leverage,\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00007",
      "start_char": 1238,
      "end_char": 1470,
      "text": "and no sign of stretched asset prices.\n36. Bank loans and the financial health of non-financial corporations should be monitored\nin the context of the recent rapid rise of interest rates. The increase itself should, all else equal,\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00008",
      "start_char": 1470,
      "end_char": 1659,
      "text": "increase banks’ net interest margin, especially for larger institutions. On the other hand, with\nMexican banks having increased their holdings of domestic government bonds, they could face\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00009",
      "start_char": 1659,
      "end_char": 1865,
      "text": "mark to market losses on their holdings (although the impact is not expected to be large since their\nholdings are of short duration). A large fraction of non-financial corporate credit is floating rate, so\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00010",
      "start_char": 1865,
      "end_char": 2067,
      "text": "higher interest payments could strain liquidity for some corporates and possibly increase default\nrates. Offsetting the pressures from higher interest rates, Mexican corporates retain relatively strong\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00011",
      "start_char": 2067,
      "end_char": 2277,
      "text": "buffers and FSAP stress tests imply that heightened default risks under the adverse scenario from\ncurrently low levels would be manageable for the banking sector.\nNFC Average Loan Rate and 6-month Average of 5\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00012",
      "start_char": 2277,
      "end_char": 2486,
      "text": "Total Bank Lending to Non-Financial Corporates\nYear TIIESwap\n(Percent of GDP)\n(Percent) 200\n10\n9 160\n8\n120\n7\n6 80\n5\n40\nNon-financial corporate average loan rate\n4\n6-month rolling average of 5-yr TIIE swap\n0\n3\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00013",
      "start_char": 2486,
      "end_char": 2555,
      "text": "Sources: Bloomberg and BIS Statistics.\nINTERNATIONAL MONETARY FUND 19"
    }
  ]
}

{
  "schema_version": "locator_index_v1",
  "source_id": "MAC-010",
  "source_sha256": "8fac4146590dbe2ed458f7356748f5880d021e03cc4982b1e7efe083d33f622a",
  "locator": "page 24",
  "segments": [
    {
      "segment_id": "S00001",
      "start_char": 0,
      "end_char": 198,
      "text": "MEXICO\n37. The authorities should take steps to further enhance the resilience of the financial\nsystem. The authorities have already rolled out critical Basel reforms, including the introduction of\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00002",
      "start_char": 198,
      "end_char": 378,
      "text": "Total Loss Absorbing Capacity (TLAC) requirements and improved supervisory techniques and\nmethodologies. The 2022 FSAP offers several recommendations for further steps (Annex XI).\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00003",
      "start_char": 378,
      "end_char": 595,
      "text": "• Capacity of regulatory agencies. The autonomy and resources of regulatory government\nagencies and the legal protection of supervisors could be strengthened, to equip them to deal\nwith the evolving risk environment.\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00004",
      "start_char": 595,
      "end_char": 767,
      "text": "• Banking supervision and regulation. The banking regulator (CNBV) should continue to\nimprove supervisory techniques, by simplifying its risk-based rating system and using\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00005",
      "start_char": 767,
      "end_char": 947,
      "text": "methodologies that are principles- rather than rules-based. It should also be enabled to\nsupervise effectively all financial conglomerates on a consolidated basis. Currently, such\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00006",
      "start_char": 947,
      "end_char": 1176,
      "text": "conglomerates can voluntarily request authorization to operate as a financial group, but some\nhave not done so. The authorities should amend the 2014 Financial Groups law to make\napplication mandatory for all such conglomerates.\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00007",
      "start_char": 1176,
      "end_char": 1357,
      "text": "• Systemic liquidity management. System-wide liquidity risks appear contained, with\ncommercial banks well-placed to provide liquidity to other financial institutions during periods\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00008",
      "start_char": 1357,
      "end_char": 1540,
      "text": "of stress. Moreover, the authorities have demonstrated the effectiveness of their toolkit to\nsupport systemic liquidity during the pandemic. But conditions bear continued monitoring,\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00009",
      "start_char": 1540,
      "end_char": 1723,
      "text": "particularly in a context of severe downside risks, and high levels of short-term wholesale\nfunding of development banks deserve further consideration, though risks are attenuated by\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00010",
      "start_char": 1723,
      "end_char": 1942,
      "text": "their liabilities guaranteed by the sovereign. Banxico’s Emergency Liquidity Assistance\nframework could be further enhanced.\n• Safety nets. CNBV should closely monitor risks from loan concentration risks and contingent\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00011",
      "start_char": 1942,
      "end_char": 2126,
      "text": "credit lines to non-financial corporates and apply Pillar 2 requirements as needed. Bank\nresolution and recovery could be strengthened by removing impediments to banks’ resolvability,\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00012",
      "start_char": 2126,
      "end_char": 2317,
      "text": "eliminating barriers to the effective use of the purchase and assumption and bridge bank tools,\nand expanding the resolution regime’s remit to financial holding companies. Board vacancies at\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00013",
      "start_char": 2317,
      "end_char": 2483,
      "text": "the Deposit Insurance and Resolution Authority (IPAB) should be filled.\n• Cybersecurity. Banxico and CNBV could enhance their approach to strengthening cybersecurity\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00014",
      "start_char": 2483,
      "end_char": 2723,
      "text": "in the financial system, by further developing their strategy and enhancing oversight, inspection,\nand investigative powers and instruments.\n• Climate risks. Financial system exposure to physical and transition risks from climate change is\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00015",
      "start_char": 2723,
      "end_char": 2935,
      "text": "manageable but financial tail risks will worsen, particularly if investments in resilience are not\nmade and global climate policy action is delayed, resulting in higher corporate defaults and\nlower bank capital.\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00016",
      "start_char": 2935,
      "end_char": 3149,
      "text": "38. The macrofinancial toolkit could benefit from additional instruments. While banking\nsystem and corporate sector risks appear contained, the mortgage market is less well regulated.\n20 INTERNATIONAL MONETARY FUND"
    }
  ]
}

{
  "schema_version": "locator_index_v1",
  "source_id": "MAC-010",
  "source_sha256": "8fac4146590dbe2ed458f7356748f5880d021e03cc4982b1e7efe083d33f622a",
  "locator": "page 25",
  "segments": [
    {
      "segment_id": "S00001",
      "start_char": 0,
      "end_char": 206,
      "text": "MEXICO\nMortgages are still small as a share of bank capital and GDP, but they are growing rapidly (Figure 9).\nIntroducing loan-to-value and debt-to-income requirements at this early stage would be prudent,\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00002",
      "start_char": 206,
      "end_char": 364,
      "text": "ahead of a possible risk build-up. Publishing a macroprudential strategy and counter-cyclical buffer\nguidelines would further advance macroprudential policy.\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00003",
      "start_char": 364,
      "end_char": 556,
      "text": "39. Financial deepening should remain a policy priority. The authorities have undertaken\nvarious efforts in recent years, including to increase access to bank branches and financial products,\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00004",
      "start_char": 556,
      "end_char": 751,
      "text": "improve transparency and broaden access with more digital connectivity. These efforts should\ncontinue. Mexico’s relatively poor financial depth is a headwind to inclusive growth. Difficulties in\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00005",
      "start_char": 751,
      "end_char": 924,
      "text": "collateral recovery and perceptions of judicial quality are also headwinds (see below).\n40. The authorities should continue to foster the development of Fintech to increase\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00006",
      "start_char": 924,
      "end_char": 1118,
      "text": "competition in the financial sector and broaden financial inclusion. Mexico’s 2018 Fintech law\nwas an important step in developing the digital financial services industry, and helped foster the\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00007",
      "start_char": 1118,
      "end_char": 1314,
      "text": "rapid creation of new firms, primarily in the e-payments sector. Additionally, Banxico has recently\nundertaken a plan to launch a central bank digital currency (CBDC). A primary objective of this\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00008",
      "start_char": 1314,
      "end_char": 1513,
      "text": "initiative is to promote greater financial inclusion. As CBDC implementation raises complex legal,\nregulatory, and operational issues with little global experience so far, Banxico should continue to\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00009",
      "start_char": 1513,
      "end_char": 1673,
      "text": "engage with stakeholders and ensure sufficient resources are allocated, including to ensure that\nsafeguards to financial stability and integrity remain robust.\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00010",
      "start_char": 1673,
      "end_char": 1842,
      "text": "41. The authorities should build on their good progress in aligning the legal and\nregulatory framework with the Financial Action Task Force (FATF) standard by enhancing\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00011",
      "start_char": 1842,
      "end_char": 2044,
      "text": "effectiveness. Efforts should continue to ensure that adequate, accurate and timely information on\nbeneficial ownership is available. The authorities’ plans to establish a beneficial ownership register\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00012",
      "start_char": 2044,
      "end_char": 2225,
      "text": "and strengthen the legal framework applicable to designated non-financial business and professions\nare key elements of this endeavor. Efforts should remain to strengthen Anti-Money\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00013",
      "start_char": 2225,
      "end_char": 2415,
      "text": "Laundering/Combating the Financing of Terrorism (AML/CFT) consolidated supervision, allocate\nadequate resources and reinforce enforcement to ensure effective, proportionate, consistent, and\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00014",
      "start_char": 2415,
      "end_char": 2630,
      "text": "dissuasive sanctions. Emerging financial integrity risks, including those related to fintech and virtual\nassets (i.e., registration, customer due diligence, and supervision) also need monitoring.\nAuthorities’ Views\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00015",
      "start_char": 2630,
      "end_char": 2804,
      "text": "42. The authorities underscored their commitment to monitoring and containing\nemerging systemic risks in the context of their risk-based prudential oversight. They concurred\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00016",
      "start_char": 2804,
      "end_char": 2993,
      "text": "that the Mexican financial system is robust and resilient to possible future adverse shocks. They\nshared the view that the policy framework performed well during the pandemic shock and are\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00017",
      "start_char": 2993,
      "end_char": 3189,
      "text": "considering further analysis of potential system-wide liquidity risks, as new global shocks emerge.\nThey intend to continue strengthening the risk-based supervisory framework and take note of the\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00018",
      "start_char": 3189,
      "end_char": 3379,
      "text": "recommendations to expand the application of the consolidated supervision framework. As for\nother potential risks and recommendations, the authorities did not fully share the concern on the\n"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "S00019",
      "start_char": 3379,
      "end_char": 3603,
      "text": "risks associated to contingent credit lines (the vast majority of such lines are revocable) or on\nliquidity risks from development banks (as these institutions are fully backed by the sovereign\nINTERNATIONAL MONETARY FUND 21"
    }
  ]
}
